summaries.summary:
The philosophical study of terrorism has two connected but distinct tasks: clarifying the concept and evaluating its morality. In ordinary and philosophical usage, terrorism is generally understood as violence, or a threat of violence, intended to produce fear and thereby coerce people toward a further objective. Disagreement concerns whether actual violence is required, whether property may be targeted, whether the objective must be political, and whether the actor must be a non-state group. The most important dispute is between wide and narrow definitions. Wide definitions cover much of the historical usage, including state and revolutionary violence, and avoid building innocence into the concept. Narrow definitions restrict terrorism to violence against non-combatants or innocent people. They are useful in moral discussion because they distinguish attacks on ordinary civilians from political assassination or attacks on combatants, though they may be somewhat revisionary and carry a presumption against the practice.

The history of the term shows why labels are contested. It originally referred to the Jacobin Reign of Terror during the French Revolution. In the nineteenth century, revolutionary groups defended dramatic violence as “propaganda by the deed,” while later totalitarian states used unpredictable political terror against populations. In modern conflicts, insurgents often describe themselves as freedom fighters and accuse governments of being the true terrorists; governments commonly reverse that description. This opposing use of labels can block rational debate.

